The code statements that lead to the bug are:
```c
watchdog_enable(cpu);
watchdog_disable(cpu);
```
Reason:
The unconditional invocation of `watchdog_enable` and `watchdog_disable` during CPU hotplug operations caused the watchdog to be activated or deactivated on CPUs that were not part of the `watchdog_allowed_mask`, leading to the watchdog becoming unstoppable on those CPUs.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
```c
if (cpumask_test_cpu(cpu, &watchdog_allowed_mask))
    watchdog_enable(cpu);
```
```c
if (cpumask_test_cpu(cpu, &watchdog_allowed_mask))
    watchdog_disable(cpu);
```
Reason:
By wrapping the calls to `watchdog_enable` and `watchdog_disable` in a conditional check that verifies if the CPU is part of the `watchdog_allowed_mask`, the patch ensures that these functions are only invoked for CPUs that are intended to be monitored by the watchdog, thus preventing the watchdog from becoming unstoppable on unintended CPUs.

